{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "interaction_emergence",
      "parent_relation": "The environment control eta rescales a single screened nonlocal correction built from one common rational saturation of sin(kappa/2)^2 + chi*sin(kappa)^2, so all eta-dependent shape changes are tied to one fixed mixture set by chi.",
      "child_relation": "Relax the assumption that the two reciprocal-space harmonics experience the environment identically, so eta changes the relative weight of the sin(kappa/2)^2 and sin(kappa)^2 contributions through distinct screening of short- and longer-range effective couplings.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "kappa",
        "eta"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "At fixed chi, increasing eta changes not only the overall curvature enhancement but also the kappa-location and sharpness of mid-zone flattening/steepening; a single parent parameter set cannot match two conditions where the small-k slope enhancement is similar but the mid-zone shape differs."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "halfzone",
        "expression": "sin(kappa/2)**2",
        "role": "fundamental acoustic lattice harmonic controlling the nearest-zone turnover"
      },
      {
        "name": "fullzone",
        "expression": "sin(kappa)**2",
        "role": "higher reciprocal-space harmonic associated with more extended effective coupling"
      },
      {
        "name": "mixratio",
        "expression": "sin(kappa)**2/(sin(kappa/2)**2 + 1.0e-12)",
        "role": "wavevector-dependent leverage of the extended-harmonic contribution relative to the fundamental one"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Visible for moderate nonlocal correction eta not asymptotically small and for wavevectors away from the strict acoustic limit, especially intermediate zone fractions where both sin(kappa/2)^2 and sin(kappa)^2 are appreciable; if eta is tiny or kappa is near 0 or the zone edge only, the contrast is weak.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, eta-dependent curvature changes already appear; in holdout, the same mechanism extends to condition-dependent redistribution between low-k and mid-zone dispersion shape rather than a uniform family controlled by one fixed harmonic mixture.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law when the two harmonic channels share the same environment response, i.e. in the limit of identical screening/saturation for both contributions or when the extra distinction parameter tends to the value enforcing a common denominator/weighting.",
    "freedom_change": "parameter_to_input",
    "promoted_symbol": "chi",
    "promotion_reason": "With chi fixed, the parent forces one environment-independent harmonic mixture across all conditions. Releasing chi as a sampled condition allows physically admissible environment-dependent redistribution between reciprocal-space harmonics without adding a new mechanism outside the existing nonlocal harmonic picture.",
    "gap_statement": "The current model assumes that all environment dependence enters through one shared screened combination of the two lattice harmonics, so the relative importance of the extended-harmonic component is fixed across conditions.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The additional reciprocal-space harmonic weighted by chi responds to the environment with the same effective screening law as the fundamental nonlocal harmonic contribution.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Different effective further-neighbor force channels generally have different spatial decay or screening lengths, so the environment can alter the projected Fourier weights of distinct lattice harmonics rather than merely scaling one fixed mixture.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "kappa",
      "eta",
      "zeta",
      "chi"
    ],
    "rationale": "Parent response relationship: Omega is |sin(kappa/2)| times a square-root correction whose eta dependence is carried by a single rational function of a fixed harmonic combination sin(kappa/2)^2 + chi*sin(kappa)^2. Thus, once chi is fixed, varying eta can only strengthen or saturate one predetermined shape family. Child change: allow the relative contribution of the higher harmonic to vary with condition, representing differential screening of coupling channels with different spatial range. Distinguishing condition: vary eta and the promoted condition chi across admissible environments while holding the lattice spacing, nearest-neighbor reference stiffness, and zeta fixed; then compare two cases with similar low-k acoustic behavior but different intermediate-k curvature or shoulder strength. The parent with one shared parameter set cannot reproduce this because its eta dependence is separable through one fixed harmonic mixture, so matching the low-k trend also locks the mid-zone redistribution. This is a changed target mapping, not merely an auxiliary reinterpretation, because the scored Omega(kappa,eta,chi) surface itself gains a condition-dependent interaction between eta and harmonic content. Scientifically admissible activation requires non-negligible effective nonlocal coupling and measurements spanning intermediate kappa; if only very small kappa are observed, both parent and child collapse toward the same acoustic limit and the refinement is weak."
  },
